<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Corcyra</h1><p class="st-meta">734 BCE – 229 BCE (approx.) · capital Corcyra</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Corinthian colony on Corfu that grew into a major naval power, whose clash with Corinth helped trigger the Peloponnesian War.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Corinthians under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chersicrates">Chersicrates</a> founded Corcyra on the island of Corfu c. 733 BCE, displacing earlier Eretrian settlers. Its position on the route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> made it rich, and it soon broke with its mother city: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> dates the first Greek sea battle, between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/corinth">Corinth</a> and Corcyra, to c. 664 BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Corcyra had the second-largest Greek navy by the 430s BCE. Its quarrel with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/corinth">Corinth</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Epidamnus</a> led to battles at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leucimme">Leukimme</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sybota">Sybota</a> and an alliance with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a>. A brutal civil war between democrats and oligarchs from 427 BCE is described by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a>. The temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artemis">Artemis</a> has the oldest stone Greek pediment, with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgon">Gorgon</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> Corcyra was held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agathocles-of-syracuse">Agathocles of Syracuse</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrrhus-of-epirus">Pyrrhus</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-epirus">Kingdom of Epirus</a>. After an Illyrian attack in 229 BCE, it placed itself under Roman protection, becoming a Roman ally and base.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 734 BCE – 229 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:45.09%;width:10.05%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>734 BCE – 229 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Corcyra</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion; cult of Artemis</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Doric Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kerkyra, Corfu</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-482&amp;focus=corcyra" aria-label="Corcyra on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5170 760 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Corcyra lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5490 1016L5572 1070M5490 1016L5519 1030M5490 1016L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5572 1070h0M5519 1030h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5490 1016h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5490 1016h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-482&amp;focus=corcyra">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
